    summary: `# 🧬 Rec Adv Bioscience #5.1 — GI Protozoa

## 🎯 Major GI protozoa in vet

| Protozoan | Hosts | Disease | Diagnostic |
|---|---|---|---|
| Giardia | Dog, cat, calf, all | Diarrhea, malabsorption | Cyst in feces, ELISA |
| Cryptosporidium | Calf, lamb, all | Watery diarrhea | Acid-fast oocyst |
| Coccidia (Eimeria, Isospora) | Many | Bloody diarrhea | Oocyst (sporulated) |
| Entamoeba | Primarily human | Dysentery | Trophozoite/cyst |
| Balantidium coli | Pigs | Mostly commensal | Large ciliate |
| Tritrichomonas foetus | Cats, cattle | Diarrhea (cat), repro (cattle) | PCR, culture |

## 🪱 Giardia (most common!)

### Life cycle
- Trophozoite (active) in intestine, multiplies
- Cyst (infective, environmental form) shed in feces
- Fecal-oral, water-borne, zoonotic

### Clinical
- Acute or chronic diarrhea
- Soft stool, no blood typically
- Malabsorption (B12, fat)
- Failure to thrive (puppies, kittens, calves)
- Often asymptomatic carrier

### Diagnosis
- **Fecal flotation (zinc sulfate or sucrose)** — cyst
- **Direct smear** — trophozoite (fresh diarrhea)
- **ELISA antigen** (SNAP Giardia) — sensitive
- **PCR** — research, genotyping (assemblages A-H)

### Treatment
- Metronidazole (most common)
- Fenbendazole (also dewormer, dual purpose)
- Combination if refractory

## 🪱 Cryptosporidium

### Life cycle
- Sporulated oocyst (immediately infective!)
- Fecal-oral, water-borne (chlorine-resistant!)
- Zoonotic (calf handlers especially)

### Clinical
- Calves 1-3 weeks: profuse watery diarrhea
- Severe dehydration
- Self-limiting in immunocompetent (~7-14 days)
- AIDS humans: chronic, fatal

### Diagnosis
- **Acid-fast stain** of fecal smear (red oocyst, ~5 μm)
- **Modified ZN, Auramine** — easier to see
- **ELISA antigen**
- **PCR**

### Treatment
- No fully effective treatment
- Halofuginone (preventive in calves)
- Nitazoxanide (humans)
- Supportive (oral electrolytes, IV fluids)

## 🪱 Coccidiosis

### Eimeria (cattle, sheep, goat, poultry)
- Multiple species, host-specific
- Bloody diarrhea (esp. *E. zuernii*, *E. bovis*, *E. tenella* poultry)
- Tx: amprolium, sulfa, monensin (poultry feed)

### Isospora suis (pigs)
- Suckling pig 5-15 days
- Yellow pasty diarrhea
- Tx: toltrazuril (Baycox) — single oral dose at 4 days

### Cystoisospora canis, felis (dogs, cats)
- Puppies, kittens
- Diarrhea, usually mild
- Tx: ponazuril, sulfa

### Diagnosis
- Fecal flotation — sporulated oocysts
- Species ID by oocyst size + shape (challenging)
- McMaster count for severity (poultry, ruminants)

## 🪱 Tritrichomonas foetus
- **Cats:** large bowel diarrhea (cattery), Bengal/Siamese
- **Cattle:** veneral, infertility, abortion (rare in TH)
- Dx: cat PCR (preferred), pouch culture
- Tx: ronidazole (cats — extra-label, careful dosing)

## 🪱 Histomonas (poultry — blackhead disease)
- Heterakis (cecal worm) carries protozoan
- Turkeys severely affected (cecum + liver)
- Tx: limited (nitarsone banned), prevention via deworming

## 🩺 Approach to diarrhea in animals

### Algorithm
1. History (age, diet, environment)
2. Fecal exam:
   - Direct (fresh, motile)
   - Flotation (cyst, oocyst, egg)
   - Sedimentation (heavy ova)
3. Antigen ELISA (Giardia, Crypto)
4. PCR panel (multiplex GI)
5. Culture (bacterial, virus)
6. Histopath if biopsy

## 📝 EXAM RECAP
1. ⭐ Giardia: cyst in feces, metronidazole/fenbendazole
2. ⭐ Cryptosporidium: acid-fast oocyst, calf handler zoonosis
3. ⭐ Isospora suis: pigs 5-15 d, toltrazuril
4. ⭐ Tritrichomonas: cat diarrhea, ronidazole
5. ⭐ Histomonas: turkey blackhead, no good Rx`,
